\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{Biochar-Amended Biofilters for Car Park Drainage:\newline A Nine-Month Column Study}
\author{L.~Brandt, M.~Osei, and T.~Kowal}
\date{March 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Six laboratory biofilter columns treated synthetic stormwater runoff for nine months.
Biochar-amended media removed 42~\% of the nitrate load at a residence time of 38 minutes.
The columns sustained an outflow rate of 1.8~L per hour without clogging.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Stormwater runoff from car parks carries nitrate, metals, and tyre wear particles into urban streams.
Biofilters with amended media offer a low-maintenance treatment step before discharge.
Most published column studies ran for a single season and used clean sand media.
The present study ran for nine months and dosed the columns with synthetic run-off from car park catchments.

\section{Column Setup}
Six acrylic columns of 15~cm diameter were packed to a depth of 60~cm.
Four columns received media amended with 10~\% biochar by volume; two control columns received sand only.
The synthetic runoff recipe followed the county monitoring record for car park drainage.
An orifice plate controlled the outflow rate at 1.8~L per hour.
The retention time of each column was checked weekly with a bromide tracer.

\section{Hydraulic Operation}
The nominal residence time of each column was 38 minutes at the design flow.
Residence time was computed from the saturated pore volume and the inflow rate.
Dosing of synthetic runoff followed a weekly wet-dry cycle.
The outflow rate of each column was logged at one-minute resolution and averaged hourly.
No column showed surface ponding at any point during the nine months.

\section{Water Quality}
Effluent samples were drawn twice a week and analysed for nitrate and zinc.
The effluent concentration of nitrate fell by 42~\% over the nine months.
Zinc removal reached 71~\% in the amended columns and 33~\% in the controls.
Columns with longer retention time showed the highest nitrate removal.

\section{Conclusion}
Biochar amendment doubled nitrate removal relative to sand-only controls.
Effluent quality remained stable through the winter dosing period.
Treating run-off at source with amended biofilters is practical for car park drainage of this scale.
\end{document}
